From fb0346c9af4dbf07751002f88e1654ad21006c38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 23:43:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] lowering: GCC-exact dynamic layout for structs with VLA
 members

Three lowering defects composed into wrong member addressing for GCC's
VLA-in-struct extension (gcc.c-torture/execute align-nest.c segfaulted
at -O2/-O3 on x86-64; pr82210.c aborted on every -O level):

1. compute_vla_field_offsets_for_decl skipped bitfields entirely, so a
   bitfield following the VLA member never advanced the dynamic offset
   cursor (align-nest: packed { int i[n]; unsigned b:1; myint mi; }
   recorded mi at 4n instead of the GCC-exact 4n+1), and it recomputed
   the pre-VLA prefix by hand instead of seeding from the static
   layout's offset of the VLA member (bitfields before the VLA member
   were lost). Members after a second VLA member were never recorded at
   all (pr82210: struct S { T a[size]; int b[size]; } never recorded
   b's align_up(a_bytes, 4) offset, so s.b[i] addressed element 0).

2. compute_vla_struct_sizeof likewise skipped bitfields and ignored the
   struct-level __attribute__((aligned(N))): sizeof gave 4n+4 instead
   of align_up(4n+5, 4) = 16 for align-nest with n=2. The wrong runtime
   sizeof fed the s[k] element stride and the DynAlloca size.

3. compute_vla_runtime_size returned None for constant-dimension arrays
   whose ELEMENT type is dynamically sized, so 'struct S s[2]' got a
   static alloca sized from the FAM-based static sizeof (2*8=16 bytes)
   while member addressing used the runtime stride -- the k=1 store
   wrote past the object and, with a tight -O2 frame, over the return
   address. Arrays of VLA-member structs now size as count x sizeof(S).

Both walkers now share VlaDynCursor, a byte/bit cursor that seeds at the
VLA member's static offset, advances bit-granularly for packed
bitfields, applies the SysV straddle rule for unpacked bitfields when
the cursor's alignment guarantee makes it statically decidable (fresh
aligned unit otherwise), rounds trailing bitfield runs to the storage
unit end, and applies struct-level aligned(N) exactly like
resolve_struct_or_union. Field alignment mirrors
StructLayoutBuilder::compute_field_alignment including per-field packed/
aligned attributes. Unpackable shapes stay self-consistent and
overlap-free.

get_struct_base_addr resolved nested member addresses through the static
layout only, so s[k].mi.value stored through static offset 1 while
&s[k].mi (the lvalue address-of path) used the dynamic offset -- the two
paths disagreed by 8 bytes in the same expression. Both MemberAccess and
PointerMemberAccess arms now prefer the recorded dynamic offset.

Also fixed the parser leak that made pr82210 miscompile: an alignment
attribute parsed BEFORE a nested struct definition
('__attribute__((aligned(16))) struct T { short c; } a[size];') stayed
pending while the struct body was parsed and landed on the body's first
field, inflating sizeof(struct T) from 2 to 16. parse_struct_or_union
now saves the pending _Alignas/aligned value across body parsing and
restores it for the declarator, matching GCC (attributes after the
closing brace still apply to the type itself).

Verification: align-nest.c and pr82210.c pass at -O0..-O3 on both
backends with layout probes byte-identical to GCC; amd64 torture -O3
1680/1692 (0 run-fail, was 1679/1); i686 torture -O3 1678/1692 (0
run-fail, was 1677/1); cargo unit 1480/0/6; amd64 regression suite
555/0/15; i686 regression suite 483/80/15 (+1, fails all pre-existing
i686 gaps).
